ect\n107: :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\n108: :yin.k/frame       {…}                        ; §7.4  the canonical CESK at a safepoint\n114: :yin.k/carried     [ … ]                      ; §7.3.4  canonical instruction vectors shipped inline\n115: :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive}          ; §7.7  or :yin.k/fork\n123:(`:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, with the custody facts of §7.7 on\n136:`dao.jing` segment address of the map with `:yin.k/id` removed (§7.3.4).\n144:## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\n146:### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\n152:### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction vector and its address\n183:contract stamp (§7.3.3) and is versioned with it.\n259:### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\n268:- the tuple grammar of §7.3.2 — the mnemonic set, each mnemonic's tuple\n303:transition; see the revision log in S7.11. The de Bruijn images carry\n319:### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\n325:§7.3.2 normalizes away.\n344:obligation: both must yield the same image, and a test in the harness (§7.11)\n389:snapshot variant and every retry of the same park (§7.7.2). A re-emission\n393:## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\n395:### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\n440: :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except where §7.4.3 pre-fills it\n446: :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\n456:effect that misses the module registry (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2, 7.3); a\n471:| :link-response | the request append's ok; the kept      | pc+1      | St minus the popped    | a matching refusal, or a failed                  |\n481:The pending variants of the three reasons are in section 7.4.3.\n488:### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\n545:### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n551:the set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\n581: :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n586:- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n589:  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n593:  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n601:  assigning new positions. The cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\n602:  attaches a reflection through that descriptor and resumes at the kept\n640:safepoint of the same name (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2, 7.3). A wait entry's\n641:`:cursor` (the kept cursor) is UCF's `:yin.k/cell`: the cell id stands for\n642:the cursor and its kept position rides in the cells table of section\n661: :yin.k/cell      :yin.k/c-31}            ; the kept cell, at its kept\n678:envelope, `:link-response` carries the response descriptor and the kept\n696:## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n698:### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\n720:| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n759:of the module store its body resolves against (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3):\n779:lower-side refusal (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3), not a `:yin.k/non-portable`\n784:relative to it. `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3 states each kernel's lift and\n794:out of the store (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). Lowering installs an attached\n822:### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\n874:  publication is an acceptance blocker (S7.11).\n877:  `:define` transition (S7.3.3), which writes its literal key through\n885:### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n913:a single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n941:§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n946:split of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3: each cell becomes a cursor entry in the\n961:a kept position. A raw host handle\n967:### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\n983:(`:non-canonicalizable` and `:foreign-parked-ref` from §7.3.2 and §7.6.3;\n984:the last three from linker M4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). There is no partial UCF value and no\n992:## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\n994:### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively\n1031:  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\n1069:### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\n1085:its own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\n1089:amended by the linker's resource split (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3): stream\n1113:keys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n1122:them (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). One snapshot encodes per module, under its\n1138:`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3 states.\n1140:### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\n1152:  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\n1154:  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\n1172:### 7.6.4 Modules\n1185:### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\n1206:`dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive, module, or cursor profile\n1213:## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\n1215:### 7.7.1 The race, named\n1230:### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\n1255:transition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\n1262:(§7.3.4).\n1264:### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n1279:**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\n1305:recorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\n1310:### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n1332:   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n1353:and **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\n1360:### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n1376:(7.7.3); only the possessing boundary writes it. Exactly-once admission\n1387:has changed, such as a kept cursor returning `gap` after eviction. The\n1423:queryable history (7.7.6); a holder report is evidence, while the grantor\n1442:### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n1455:recomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\n1458:### 7.7.7 Restart\n1468:## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n1474:2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n1477:3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n1479:   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n1481:   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n1484:   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n1487:   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n1505:   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n1511:   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n1514:   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n1522:   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n1524:   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\n1530:## §7.9 Outcome algebra\n1559:## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n1579:## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n1595:  windows of §7.7.5.\n1596:- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n1598:  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n1601:  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, which preserves the source stream's kept cursor\n1615:  (§7.3.3): the tuple grammar — mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and operand\n1639:  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\n1643:  static `stack-effect`/`lexically-required` checks, and the §7.3.4\n1647:  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\n1649:### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\n1660:**Code identity (7.3; blocker 5).**\n1697:**Safepoint reconstruction (7.4; blocker 1).**\n1701:- Setup: Run the corpus to every 7.4.1 row: explicit park, blocked\n1706:  at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n1726:  continuation holding a string-backed local `dao.stream` with a kept\n1729:  descriptor is a remote descriptor and the cell carries the kept\n1731:  stream. Reads resume at the kept position. Force retention loss and\n1741:  identity; the kept-cursor proof no longer waits for post-M5 hardening.\n1777:  M4 also proves the string-backed stream reflection and kept-cursor case in\n1781:**Ownership arbitration (7.7; blocker 4).**\n1791:  release. After a crash, evict a kept-cursor value so the re-granted run\n16:## 1. Purpose, referent, and validity rule\n51:## 2. What one revision names (the referent)\n80:## 3. Design-phase revisions (before UCF execution)\n134:## 4. In-tree publication and amendments\n182:## 5. Implementation and integration records\n224:  of UCF 7.4.2, plus `:yin.safepoint/reasons` (ucf.cljc:351),\n254:  including the `:reasons` finding (section 8 here) and the\n258:  P2 -- the revision history (this document); P3 -- `:reasons`\n305:## 6. Pending amendments named but not landed\n322:## 7. Source disagreements and owner rulings\n367:7.4 The static reasons field. UCF 7.4.2's published static map lists\n369:`/lexically-required`, `/layout` -- no reasons field. Phase 1's\n370:`safepoints` publishes `:yin.safepoint/reasons` (ucf.cljc:351)\n371:anyway, described as \"the reasons under which the machine parks\n391:## 8. Owner ruling: static kinds, dynamic reasons\n399:`:yin.safepoint/reasons` field. The Phase 1 field exceeds that map.\n407:not observed reasons. They must not be copied into `:yin.k/reason`.\n409:M4 shall replace `:yin.safepoint/reasons` with\n423:execution revision: `:yin.safepoint/reasons` was not a component named\n439:classifier or mint new wait-entry reasons.\n455:## 9. Source index\n50:## 1. Overview and scope\n83:## 2. The starting tree (master `c66809fa`)\n137:## 3. Rulings\n176:## 4. The content source\n178:### 4.1 Composition\n218:### 4.2 The module closure walker\n296:### 4.3 The staged load\n380:## 5. Publication\n382:### 5.1 Rows, every round (owner decision 1)\n421:### 5.2 A module closure\n497:### 5.3 Deriving a module from indexed code (R9)\n558:### 5.4 Announcing\n566:### 5.5 Replication: the ledger, the backlog, repair and the result\n573:#### 5.5.1 The publication ledger\n614:#### 5.5.2 The backlog\n673:#### 5.5.3 The result and the events\n737:#### 5.5.4 Automatic repair (owner decision 5: \"Automatic retry while open\")\n801:#### 5.5.5 Cancellation\n840:#### 5.5.6 What the result means for the index and for readers\n860:## 6. Names: envelopes, proofs, keys\n862:### 6.1 The envelope\n889:### 6.2 The principal id and key encoding\n903:### 6.3 The proof and the signed bytes\n941:### 6.4 `yin.vm.linker.sign`\n975:### 6.5 The key file (owner decision 2)\n1035:### 6.6 Republishing a name\n1044:## 7. Resolution\n1046:### 7.1 Authority is the reader's composition\n1060:### 7.2 The snapshot set\n1071:### 7.3 The fold\n1114:### 7.4 Transitive requires\n1171:## 8. Evaluation\n1173:### 8.1 Entry\n1183:### 8.2 Pending and the re-check (R11)\n1202:### 8.3 A request budget is not a lease (R12)\n1212:### 8.4 Implicit network (R10)\n1219:## 9. Failure vocabulary\n1304:## 10. The plain Clojure API\n1403:## 11. Limits and defaults\n1436:## 12. Slices\n1759:## 13. Deferrals\n1794:## Lineage\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
